  "result": "CG3 (https://datasheets.scbt.com/sc-205990.pdf)\nciteturn131view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 9; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn115view2\",\"lineno\":18}); Total lines: 500\nL0@P0: Material Safety Data Sheet\nL1@P0: Vitamin K3\nL2@P0: sc-205990\nL3@P0: Hazard Alert Code Key: EXTREME HIGH MODERATE LOW\nL4@P0: Section 1 - CHEMICAL PRODUCT AND COMPANY IDENTIFICATION\nL5@P0: PRODUCT NAME\nL6@P0: Vitamin K3\nL7@P0: STATEMENT OF HAZARDOUS NATURE\nL8@P0: CONSIDERED A HAZARDOUS SUBSTANCE ACCORDING TO OSHA 29 CFR 1910.1200.\nL9@P0: NFPA\nL10@P0: SUPPLIER\nL11@P0: Santa Cruz Biotechnology, Inc.\nL12@P0: 2145 Delaware Avenue\nL13@P0: Santa Cruz, California 95060\nL14@P0: 800.457.3801 or 831.457.3800\nL15@P0: EMERGENCY:\nL16@P0: ChemWatch\nL17@P0: Within the US & Canada: 877-715-9305\nL18@P0: Outside the US & Canada: +800 2436 2255\nL19@P0: (1-800-CHEMCALL) or call +613 9573 3112\nL20@P0: SYNONYMS\nL21@P0: C11-H8-O2, \"2-methyl-1, 4-naphthalenedione\", \"2-methyl-1, 4-naphthoquinone\", \"3-methyl-1, 4-naphthaquinone\", \"Vitamin K2(0)\", Aquakay,\nL22@P0: Aquinone, Hemodal, Juva-K, Kaergona, kanone, kappaxan, Kappaxin, Karcon, Kareon, Kativ-G, Kayklot, Kaykot, Kayquinone, Kipca, \"Kipca,\nL23@P0: oil soluble\", Klottone, Koaxin, Kolklot, K-Thrombyl, K-Vitan, Menadion, Menaphthon, Menapthone, Menaquinone, Menaphtone, Mitenon,\nL24@P0: Mitenone, MNQ, NSC-4170, Prokayvit, Synkay, Thyloquinone, USAF-5185, \"natural product\"\nL25@P0: Section 2 - HAZARDS IDENTIFICATION\nL26@P0: CHEMWATCH HAZARD RATINGS\nL27@P0: Min Max\nL28@P0: Flammability: 1\nL29@P0: Toxicity: 2\nL30@P0: Body Contact: 2\nL31@P0: Reactivity: 1\nL32@P0: Chronic: 3\nL33@P0: Min/Nil=0\nL34@P0: Low=1\nL35@P0: Moderate=2\nL36@P0: High=3\nL37@P0: Extreme=4\nL38@P0: CANADIAN WHMIS SYMBOLS\nL39@P0: FLAMMABILITY 1 HEALTH HAZARD 2 INSTABILITY 0\nL40@P0-1: 1 of 9EMERGENCY OVERVIEW\nL41@P1: RISK\nL42@P1: Harmful if swallowed.\nL43@P1: Possible risk of irreversible effects.\nL44@P1: Irritating to eyes, respiratory system and skin.\nL45@P1: Very toxic to aquatic organisms, may cause long-term adverse effects in the aquatic environment.\nL46@P1: POTENTIAL HEALTH EFFECTS\nL47@P1: ACUTE HEALTH EFFECTS\nL48@P1: SWALLOWED\nL49@P1: ! Accidental ingestion of the material may be harmful; animal experiments indicate that ingestion of less than 150 gram may be fatal or may\nL50@P1: produce serious damage to the health of the individual.\nL51@P1: EYE\nL52@P1: ! This material can cause eye irritation and damage in some persons.\nL53@P1: SKIN\nL54@P1: ! This material can cause inflammation of the skin oncontact in some persons.\nL55@P1: ! The material may accentuate any pre-existing dermatitis condition.\nL56@P1: ! Open cuts, abraded or irritated skin should not be exposed to this material.\nL57@P1: ! Solution of material in moisture on the skin, or perspiration, may markedly increase skin corrosion and accelerate tissue destruction.\nL58@P1: ! Entry into the blood-stream, through, for example, cuts, abrasions or lesions, may produce systemic injury with harmful effects. Examine\nL59@P1: the skin prior to the use of the material and ensure that any external damage is suitably protected.\nL60@P1: INHALED\nL61@P1: ! The material can cause respiratory irritation in some persons. The body's response to such irritation can cause further lung damage.\nL62@P1: ! Persons with impaired respiratory function, airway diseases and conditions such as emphysema or chronic bronchitis, may incur further\nL63@P1: disability if excessive concentrations of particulate are inhaled.\nL64@P1: CHRONIC HEALTH EFFECTS\nL65@P1: ! Long-term exposure to respiratory irritants may result in disease of the airways involving difficult breathing and related systemic problems.\nL66@P1: Strong evidence exists that the substance may cause irreversible but non-lethal mutagenic effects following a single exposure.\nL67@P1: Exposure to the material may result in a possible risk of irreversible effects. The material may produce mutagenic effects in man. This\nL68@P1: concern is raised, generally, on the basis of\nL69@P1: appropriate studies using mammalian somatic cells in vivo. Such findings are often supported by positive results from in vitro mutagenicity\nL70@P1: studies.\nL71@P1: Limited evidence suggests that repeated or long-term occupational exposure may produce cumulative health effects involving organs or\nL72@P1: biochemical systems.\nL73@P1: There is some evidence that human exposure to the material may result in developmental toxicity. This evidence is based on animal studies\nL74@P1: where effects have been observed in the absence of marked maternal toxicity, or at around the same dose levels as other toxic effects but\nL75@P1: which are not secondary non-specific consequences of the other toxic effects.\nL76@P1: Menadione and it's water-soluble derivatives (such as menadiol sodium diphosphate have been reported to compete with bilirubin during\nL77@P1: their metabolism and thereby induce hyperbilirubinaemia in the neonate. Subjects deficient in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase show\nL78@P1: haemolysis which may exacerbate the hyperbilirubinaemia. Deficiency of Vitamin K produces hypoprothrombinaemia in which the clotting\nL79@P1: time of blood is prolonged and spontaneous haemorrhage may occur. Deficiency may result from poor intestinal absorption, from obstructive\nL80@P1: jaundice or severe liver disease or from the administration of coumarin or indanedione anticoagulants which interfere with Vitamin K\nL81@P1: metabolism.\nL82@P1: Quinones may undergo a reduction reaction giving rise to a semiquinone free radical. Semiquinone metabolites are highly reactive and may\nL83@P1: interact with biological macromolecules through covalent binding. They can also transfer an electron onto molecular oxygen producing\nL84@P1: superoxide radical anions, hydrogen peroxide and other reactive oxygen species.. During this reaction, the quinone is regenerated and may\nL85@P1: undergo further enzyme-catalysed one-electron reduction. A reaction cycle is continuously activated - a \"redox cycle\".\nL86@P1: Quinones may be produced from benzene, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, estrogens, and catecholamines and give rise to reactive\nL87@P1: oxygen species that can damage DNA and other cellular macromolecules and activate signaling pathways. These molecular events may be\nL88@P1: associated with the initiation, promotion, and progression of carcinogenesis\nL89@P1: The capacity of quinone derivatives to produce free radicals is largely influenced by the substituents on the molecule which in turn determine\nL90@P1: the efficiency of one electron reduction to semiquinone metabolites.\nL91@P1: There has been some concern that this material can cause cancer or mutations but there is not enough data to make an assessment.\nL92@P1: Oxygen activation (generation of a superoxide) occurs during one of the reactions of this metabolic sequence. Superoxide is a strong base\nL93@P1: and can therefore attract protons from a variety of compounds; it is also a potent reducing agent which can reduce transition metal ions (such\nL94@P1: as Fe3+ and Cu+) to their reduced form Superoxide may also act as a nucleophile and may readily react with a number of electrophilic\nL95@P1: agents. Finally superoxide may initiate oxidation reactions, for example, of molecules such as ascorbic acid or epinephrine (adrenaline)\nL96@P1: following hydrogen abstraction due to its basicity.\nL97@P1: Under certain conditions the rate of formation of reactive oxygen species may exceed the capacity of the bodies auto-oxidative defence\nL98@P1: mechanisms and, as a result, result in \"oxidative stress'\". Oxidative stress appears to be involved in some biological processes such as aging\nL99@P1: and inflammation reactions and is thought to play a role in the pathogenesis of several diseases, including acute pancreatitis, post-ischaemic\nL100@P1: syndrome,tumour formation, atherosclerosis and diabetic angiopathy.\nL101@P1: Free radicals can react with specific cellular molecules including low molecular weight biomolecules such as neurotransmitters and\nL102@P1-2: 2 of 9co-enzymes and, as a consequence, inactivate them. macromolecules and cellular membranes are particularly vulnerable to free radical\nL103@P2: damage with the resultant loss of physiological function and cell death Depolymerisation of polysaccharides (such as hyaluronic acid) may\nL104@P2: result in inflammation of the joints.\nL105@P2: Free radicals have a high affinity for sulfur containing amino-acids and therefore many proteins. The may bind covalently to these proteins\nL106@P2: leading to loss, of biological function such as catalysis exhibited by enzymes. Covalent binding may also result in allergic reactions when the\nL107@P2: modified protein is recognised, by the bodies immune system, as \"foreign\" Free radicals are also capable of causing proteins to cross-link to\nL108@P2: yield larger aggregates.\nL109@P2: Free radicals are also able to react with the nucleic acids of DNA which may affect cell division or cell death Oxidative modifications of DNA\nL110@P2: may result in tumour initiation.\nL111@P2: Lipids containing several double bonds (such as polyunsaturated fatty acids and cholesterol) are also subject to damage. In the case of\nL112@P2: membrane phospholipids, such \"peroxidation\" results in impairment of cellular and/ or subcellular membranes which may produce cell death.\nL113@P2: Transition metal ions may also play an important role in lipid peroxidation after free radical-induced change of valency . Fe3+/Fe2+, copper\nL114@P2: and mercury ions, as well as vanadate and chromate ions seem to initiate this process and may even exacerbate it by producing secondary\nL115@P2: radicals when the phospholipid is modified.\nL116@P2: Exposure to the material for prolonged periods may cause physical defects in the developing embryo (teratogenesis).\nL117@P2: Section 3 - COMPOSITION / INFORMATION ON INGREDIENTS\nL118@P2: NAME CAS RN %\nL119@P2: menadione 58-27-5 >98\nL120@P2: Section 4 - FIRST AID MEASURES\nL121@P2: SWALLOWED\nL122@P2: \" IF SWALLOWED, REFER FOR MEDICAL ATTENTION, WHERE POSSIBLE, WITHOUT DELAY. \" Where Medical attention is not\nL123@P2: immediately available or where the patient is more than 15 minutes from a hospital or unless instructed otherwise:\nL124@P2: EYE\nL125@P2: ! If this product comes in contact with the eyes: \" Wash out immediately with fresh running water. \" Ensure complete irrigation of the eye by\nL126@P2: keeping eyelids apart and away from eye and moving the eyelids by occasionally lifting the upper and lower lids.\nL127@P2: SKIN\nL128@P2: ! If skin contact occurs: \" Immediately remove all contaminated clothing, including footwear \" Flush skin and hair with running water (and\nL129@P2: soap if available).\nL130@P2: INHALED\nL131@P2: \" If fumes or combustion products are inhaled remove from contaminated area. \" Lay patient down. Keep warm and rested.\nL132@P2: NOTES TO PHYSICIAN\nL133@P2: ! for poisons (where specific treatment regime is absent):\nL134@P2: ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------BASIC TREATMENT\nL135@P2: ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nL136@P2: \" Establish a patent airway with suction where necessary.\nL137@P2: \" Watch for signs of respiratory insufficiency and assist ventilation as necessary.\nL138@P2: Treat symptomatically.\nL139@P2: Section 5 - FIRE FIGHTING MEASURES\nL140@P2: Vapour Pressure (mmHG): Negligible\nL141@P2: Upper Explosive Limit (%): Not available.\nL142@P2: Specific Gravity (water=1): Not available\nL143@P2: Lower Explosive Limit (%): Not available.\nL144@P2: EXTINGUISHING MEDIA\nL145@P2: \" Foam.\nL146@P2: \" Dry chemical powder.\nL147@P2: FIRE FIGHTING\nL148@P2: \" Alert Emergency Responders and tell them location and nature of hazard.\nL149@P2: \" Wear breathing apparatus plus protective gloves.\nL150@P2: When any large container (including road and rail tankers) is involved in a fire,\nL151@P2: consider evacuation by 100 metres in all directions.\nL152@P2: GENERAL FIRE HAZARDS/HAZARDOUS COMBUSTIBLE PRODUCTS\nL153@P2: \" Combustible solid which burns but propagates flame with difficulty.\nL154@P2: \" Avoid generating dust, particularly clouds of dust in a confined or unventilated space as dusts may form an explosive mixture with air, and\nL155@P2: any source of ignition, i.e. flame or spark, will cause fire or explosion. Dust clouds generated by the fine grinding of the solid are a particular\nL156@P2: hazard; accumulations of fine dust may burn rapidly and fiercely if ignited.\nL157@P2: Combustion products include: carbon monoxide (CO), carbon dioxide (CO2), other pyrolysis products typical of burning organic material.\nL158@P2: FIRE INCOMPATIBILITY\nL159@P2: ! Avoid contamination with oxidizing agents i.e. nitrates, oxidizing acids,chlorine bleaches, pool chlorine etc. as ignition may result.\nL160@P2-3: 3 of 9PERSONAL PROTECTION\nL161@P3: Glasses:\nL162@P3: Chemical goggles.\nL163@P3: Gloves:\nL164@P3: Respirator:\nL165@P3: Particulate\nL166@P3: Section 6 - ACCIDENTAL RELEASE MEASURES\nL167@P3: MINOR SPILLS\nL168@P3: ! Environmental hazard - contain spillage.\nL169@P3: \" Clean up waste regularly and abnormal spills immediately.\nL170@P3: \" Avoid breathing dust and contact with skin and eyes.\nL171@P3: \" Wear protective clothing, gloves, safety glasses and dust respirator.\nL172@P3: \" Use dry clean up procedures and avoid generating dust.\nL173@P3: \" Vacuum up or sweep up. NOTE: Vacuum cleaner must be fitted with an exhaust micro filter (HEPA type) (consider explosion-proof\nL174@P3: machines designed to be grounded during storage and use).\nL175@P3: \" Dampen with water to prevent dusting before sweeping.\nL176@P3: \" Place in suitable containers for disposal.\nL177@P3: MAJOR SPILLS\nL178@P3: ! Environmental hazard - contain spillage.\nL179@P3: Moderate hazard.\nL180@P3: \" CAUTION: Advise personnel in area.\nL181@P3: \" Alert Emergency Responders and tell them location and nature of hazard.\nL182@P3: Section 7 - HANDLING AND STORAGE\nL183@P3: PROCEDURE FOR HANDLING\nL184@P3: \" Avoid all personal contact, including inhalation.\nL185@P3: \" Wear protective clothing when risk of exposure occurs.\nL186@P3: Empty containers may contain residual dust which has the potential to accumulate following settling. Such dusts may explode in the presence\nL187@P3: of an appropriate ignition source.\nL188@P3: \" Do NOT cut, drill, grind or weld such containers.\nL189@P3: \" In addition ensure such activity is not performed near full, partially empty or empty containers without appropriate workplace safety\nL190@P3: authorisation or permit.\nL191@P3: RECOMMENDED STORAGE METHODS\nL192@P3: ! Glass container.\nL193@P3: \" Polyethylene or polypropylene container.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCG3 (https://datasheets.scbt.com/sc-215295.pdf)\nciteturn131view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 8; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn120view2\",\"lineno\":18}); Total lines: 467\nL0@P0: Material Safety Data Sheet\nL1@P0: Menadione sodium bisulfite\nL2@P0: sc-215295\nL3@P0: Hazard Alert Code Key: EXTREME HIGH MODERATE LOW\nL4@P0: Section 1 - CHEMICAL PRODUCT AND COMPANY IDENTIFICATION\nL5@P0: PRODUCT NAME\nL6@P0: Menadione sodium bisulfite\nL7@P0: STATEMENT OF HAZARDOUS NATURE\nL8@P0: CONSIDERED A HAZARDOUS SUBSTANCE ACCORDING TO OSHA 29 CFR 1910.1200.\nL9@P0: NFPA\nL10@P0: SUPPLIER\nL11@P0: Santa Cruz Biotechnology, Inc.\nL12@P0: 2145 Delaware Avenue\nL13@P0: Santa Cruz, California 95060\nL14@P0: 800.457.3801 or 831.457.3800\nL15@P0: EMERGENCY:\nL16@P0: ChemWatch\nL17@P0: Within the US & Canada: 877-715-9305\nL18@P0: Outside the US & Canada: +800 2436 2255\nL19@P0: (1-800-CHEMCALL) or call +613 9573 3112\nL20@P0: SYNONYMS\nL21@P0: C11-H9-Na-O5-S, \"1, 2, 3, 4-tetrahydro-2-methyl-1, 4-dioxo-2-naphthalenesulfonic acid\", sodium, \"2-methyl-1, 4-naphthoquinone sodium\nL22@P0: bisulfite\", \"menaphthone sodium bisulfate\", \"menadione sodium bisulphate\", \"sodium 1, 2, 3, 4-tetrahydro-2-methyl-1, 4-dioxo\u00022-naphthalenesulfonate\", Hemodal, Hyokinone, Ido-K, Kavitan, Klotogen, \"natural product\", \"Vitamin K analogue\"\nL23@P0: Section 2 - HAZARDS IDENTIFICATION\nL24@P0: CHEMWATCH HAZARD RATINGS\nL25@P0: Min Max\nL26@P0: Flammability: 1\nL27@P0: Toxicity: 2\nL28@P0: Body Contact: 2\nL29@P0: Reactivity: 1\nL30@P0: Chronic: 2\nL31@P0: Min/Nil=0\nL32@P0: Low=1\nL33@P0: Moderate=2\nL34@P0: High=3\nL35@P0: Extreme=4\nL36@P0: CANADIAN WHMIS SYMBOLS\nL37@P0: FLAMMABILITY 1 HEALTH HAZARD 2 INSTABILITY 0\nL38@P0-1: 1 of 8EMERGENCY OVERVIEW\nL39@P1: RISK\nL40@P1: Irritating to eyes, respiratory system and skin.\nL41@P1: Very toxic to aquatic organisms, may cause long-term adverse effects in the aquatic environment.\nL42@P1: POTENTIAL HEALTH EFFECTS\nL43@P1: ACUTE HEALTH EFFECTS\nL44@P1: SWALLOWED\nL45@P1: ! Accidental ingestion of the material may be damaging to the health of the individual.\nL46@P1: ! Ingestion of sulfite salts may cause gastric irritation. Large doses may produce violent colic, diarrhea, circulatory disturbance, depression of\nL47@P1: vital functions and, sometimes, death.\nL48@P1: EYE\nL49@P1: ! This material can cause eye irritation and damage in some persons.\nL50@P1: ! Although the material is not thought to be an irritant, direct contact with the eye may cause transient discomfort characterized by tearing or\nL51@P1: conjunctival redness (as with windburn). Slight abrasive damage may also result.\nL52@P1: <\\p>.\nL53@P1: SKIN\nL54@P1: ! The material is not thought to produce adverse health effects or skin irritation following contact (as classified using animal models).\nL55@P1: Nevertheless, good hygiene practice requires that exposure be kept to a minimum and that suitable gloves be used in an occupational\nL56@P1: setting.\nL57@P1: ! Open cuts, abraded or irritated skin should not be exposed to this material.\nL58@P1: ! Entry into the blood-stream, through, for example, cuts, abrasions or lesions, may produce systemic injury with harmful effects. Examine\nL59@P1: the skin prior to the use of the material and ensure that any external damage is suitably protected.\nL60@P1: ! This material can cause inflammation of the skin oncontact in some persons.\nL61@P1: INHALED\nL62@P1: ! The material can cause respiratory irritation in some persons. The body's response to such irritation can cause further lung damage.\nL63@P1: ! Persons with impaired respiratory function, airway diseases and conditions such as emphysema or chronic bronchitis, may incur further\nL64@P1: disability if excessive concentrations of particulate are inhaled.\nL65@P1: CHRONIC HEALTH EFFECTS\nL66@P1: ! There has been some concern that this material can cause cancer or mutations but there is not enough data to make an assessment.\nL67@P1: Limited evidence suggests that repeated or long-term occupational exposure may produce cumulative health effects involving organs or\nL68@P1: biochemical systems.\nL69@P1: Sulfites and bisulfites can cause narrowing of the airways, stomach upset, flushing, low blood pressure. tingling sensation, itchy wheal,\nL70@P1: swelling and shock, and asthmatics are especially prone.\nL71@P1: <\\p>.\nL72@P1: Quinones may undergo a reduction reaction giving rise to a semiquinone free radical. Semiquinone metabolites are highly reactive and may\nL73@P1: interact with biological macromolecules through covalent binding. They can also transfer an electron onto molecular oxygen producing\nL74@P1: superoxide radical anions, hydrogen peroxide and other reactive oxygen species.. During this reaction, the quinone is regenerated and may\nL75@P1: undergo further enzyme-catalysed one-electron reduction. A reaction cycle is continuously activated - a \"redox cycle\".\nL76@P1: Quinones may be produced from benzene, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, estrogens, and catecholamines and give rise to reactive\nL77@P1: oxygen species that can damage DNA and other cellular macromolecules and activate signaling pathways. These molecular events may be\nL78@P1: associated with the initiation, promotion, and progression of carcinogenesis\nL79@P1: The capacity of quinone derivatives to produce free radicals is largely influenced by the substituents on the molecule which in turn determine\nL80@P1: the efficiency of one electron reduction to semiquinone metabolites.\nL81@P1: Oxygen activation (generation of a superoxide) occurs during one of the reactions of this metabolic sequence. Superoxide is a strong base\nL82@P1: and can therefore attract protons from a variety of compounds; it is also a potent reducing agent which can reduce transition metal ions (such\nL83@P1: as Fe3+ and Cu+) to their reduced form Superoxide may also act as a nucleophile and may readily react with a number of electrophilic\nL84@P1: agents. Finally superoxide may initiate oxidation reactions, for example, of molecules such as ascorbic acid or epinephrine (adrenaline)\nL85@P1: following hydrogen abstraction due to its basicity.\nL86@P1: Under certain conditions the rate of formation of reactive oxygen species may exceed the capacity of the bodies auto-oxidative defence\nL87@P1: mechanisms and, as a result, result in \"oxidative stress'\". Oxidative stress appears to be involved in some biological processes such as aging\nL88@P1: and inflammation reactions and is thought to play a role in the pathogenesis of several diseases, including acute pancreatitis, post-ischaemic\nL89@P1: syndrome,tumour formation, atherosclerosis and diabetic angiopathy.\nL90@P1: Free radicals can react with specific cellular molecules including low molecular weight biomolecules such as neurotransmitters and\nL91@P1: co-enzymes and, as a consequence, inactivate them. macromolecules and cellular membranes are particularly vulnerable to free radical\nL92@P1: damage with the resultant loss of physiological function and cell death Depolymerisation of polysaccharides (such as hyaluronic acid) may\nL93@P1: result in inflammation of the joints.\nL94@P1: Free radicals have a high affinity for sulfur containing amino-acids and therefore many proteins. The may bind covalently to these proteins\nL95@P1: leading to loss, of biological function such as catalysis exhibited by enzymes. Covalent binding may also result in allergic reactions when the\nL96@P1: modified protein is recognised, by the bodies immune system, as \"foreign\" Free radicals are also capable of causing proteins to cross-link to\nL97@P1: yield larger aggregates.\nL98@P1: Free radicals are also able to react with the nucleic acids of DNA which may affect cell division or cell death Oxidative modifications of DNA\nL99@P1: may result in tumour initiation.\nL100@P1-2: 2 of 8Lipids containing several double bonds (such as polyunsaturated fatty acids and cholesterol) are also subject to damage. In the case of\nL101@P2: membrane phospholipids, such \"peroxidation\" results in impairment of cellular and/ or subcellular membranes which may produce cell death.\nL102@P2: Transition metal ions may also play an important role in lipid peroxidation after free radical-induced change of valency . Fe3+/Fe2+, copper\nL103@P2: and mercury ions, as well as vanadate and chromate ions seem to initiate this process and may even exacerbate it by producing secondary\nL104@P2: radicals when the phospholipid is modified.\nL105@P2: Menadione and it's water-soluble derivatives (such as menadiol sodium diphosphate have been reported to compete with bilirubin during\nL106@P2: their metabolism and thereby induce hyperbilirubinaemia in the neonate. Subjects deficient in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase show\nL107@P2: haemolysis which may exacerbate the hyperbilirubinaemia. Deficiency of Vitamin K produces hypoprothrombinaemia in which the clotting\nL108@P2: time of blood is prolonged and spontaneous haemorrhage may occur. Deficiency may result from poor intestinal absorption, from obstructive\nL109@P2: jaundice or severe liver disease or from the administration of coumarin or indanedione anticoagulants which interfere with Vitamin K\nL110@P2: metabolism.\nL111@P2: Section 3 - COMPOSITION / INFORMATION ON INGREDIENTS\nL112@P2: NAME CAS RN %\nL113@P2: menadione sodium bisulfite 130-37-0 >98\nL114@P2: Section 4 - FIRST AID MEASURES\nL115@P2: SWALLOWED\nL116@P2: \" If swallowed do NOT induce vomiting. \" If vomiting occurs, lean patient forward or place on left side (head-down position, if possible) to\nL117@P2: maintain open airway and prevent aspiration.\nL118@P2: EYE\nL119@P2: ! If this product comes in contact with the eyes: \" Wash out immediately with fresh running water. \" Ensure complete irrigation of the eye by\nL120@P2: keeping eyelids apart and away from eye and moving the eyelids by occasionally lifting the upper and lower lids.\nL121@P2: SKIN\nL122@P2: ! If skin or hair contact occurs: \" Flush skin and hair with running water (and soap if available). \" Seek medical attention in event of irritation.\nL123@P2: INHALED\nL124@P2: \" If dust is inhaled, remove from contaminated area. \" Encourage patient to blow nose to ensure clear passage of breathing. \" If irritation or\nL125@P2: discomfort persists seek medical attention. Inhalation of vapors or aerosols (mists, fumes) may cause lung edema. Corrosive substances\nL126@P2: may cause lung damage (e.g. <\\p>.\nL127@P2: NOTES TO PHYSICIAN\nL128@P2: ! Treat symptomatically.\nL129@P2: Depending on the degree of exposure, periodic medical examination is indicated. The symptoms of lung edema often do not manifest until a\nL130@P2: few hours have passed and they are aggravated by physical effort.\nL131@P2: <\\p>.\nL132@P2: Section 5 - FIRE FIGHTING MEASURES\nL133@P2: Vapour Pressure (mmHG): Negligible\nL134@P2: Upper Explosive Limit (%): Not available.\nL135@P2: Specific Gravity (water=1): Not available\nL136@P2: Lower Explosive Limit (%): Not available.\nL137@P2: EXTINGUISHING MEDIA\nL138@P2: \" Water spray or fog.\nL139@P2: \" Foam.\nL140@P2: FIRE FIGHTING\nL141@P2: \" Alert Emergency Responders and tell them location and nature of hazard.\nL142@P2: \" Wear breathing apparatus plus protective gloves.\nL143@P2: When any large container (including road and rail tankers) is involved in a fire,\nL144@P2: consider evacuation by 100 metres in all directions.\nL145@P2: GENERAL FIRE HAZARDS/HAZARDOUS COMBUSTIBLE PRODUCTS\nL146@P2: \" Combustible solid which burns but propagates flame with difficulty.\nL147@P2: \" Avoid generating dust, particularly clouds of dust in a confined or unventilated space as dusts may form an explosive mixture with air, and\nL148@P2: any source of ignition, i.e. flame or spark, will cause fire or explosion. Dust clouds generated by the fine grinding of the solid are a particular\nL149@P2: hazard; accumulations of fine dust may burn rapidly and fiercely if ignited.\nL150@P2: Combustion products include: carbon monoxide (CO), carbon dioxide (CO2), sulfur oxides (SOx), metal oxides, other pyrolysis products\nL151@P2: typical of burning organic material.\nL152@P2: FIRE INCOMPATIBILITY\nL153@P2: ! Avoid contamination with oxidizing agents i.e. nitrates, oxidizing acids,chlorine bleaches, pool chlorine etc. as ignition may result.\nL154@P2: PERSONAL PROTECTION\nL155@P2: Glasses:\nL156@P2: Chemical goggles.\nL157@P2-3: 3 of 8Gloves:\nL158@P3: Respirator:\nL159@P3: Particulate\nL160@P3: Section 6 - ACCIDENTAL RELEASE MEASURES\nL161@P3: MINOR SPILLS\nL162@P3: ! Environmental hazard - contain spillage.\nL163@P3: \" Clean up waste regularly and abnormal spills immediately.\nL164@P3: \" Avoid breathing dust and contact with skin and eyes.\nL165@P3: \" Wear protective clothing, gloves, safety glasses and dust respirator.\nL166@P3: \" Use dry clean up procedures and avoid generating dust.\nL167@P3: \" Vacuum up or sweep up. NOTE: Vacuum cleaner must be fitted with an exhaust micro filter (HEPA type) (consider explosion-proof\nL168@P3: machines designed to be grounded during storage and use).\nL169@P3: \" Dampen with water to prevent dusting before sweeping.\nL170@P3: \" Place in suitable containers for disposal.\nL171@P3: MAJOR SPILLS\nL172@P3: ! Environmental hazard - contain spillage.\nL173@P3: Moderate hazard.\nL174@P3: \" CAUTION: Advise personnel in area.\nL175@P3: \" Alert Emergency Responders and tell them location and nature of hazard.\nL176@P3: Section 7 - HANDLING AND STORAGE\nL177@P3: PROCEDURE FOR HANDLING\nL178@P3: \" Avoid all personal contact, including inhalation.\nL179@P3: \" Wear protective clothing when risk of exposure occurs.\nL180@P3: Empty containers may contain residual dust which has the potential to accumulate following settling. Such dusts may explode in the presence\nL181@P3: of an appropriate ignition source.\nL182@P3: \" Do NOT cut, drill, grind or weld such containers.\nL183@P3: \" In addition ensure such activity is not performed near full, partially empty or empty containers without appropriate workplace safety\nL184@P3: authorisation or permit.\nL185@P3: RECOMMENDED STORAGE METHODS\nL186@P3: ! Glass container.\nL187@P3: \" Polyethylene or polypropylene container.\nL188@P3: \" Check all containers are clearly labelled and free from leaks.\nL189@P3: STORAGE REQUIREMENTS\nL190@P3: ! Observe manufacturer's storing and handling recommendations.\nL191@P3: NOTE: Decomposes in sunlight.\nL192@P3: Section 8 - EXPOSURE CONTROLS / PERSONAL PROTECTION\nL193@P3: EXPOSURE CONTROLS\nL194@P3: Source Material TWA mg/m# Notes\nL195@P3: ___________ ___________ _______ _______\n"
}
